 for n,(v,t,why,st) in enumerate(rr,1):
  slug=f'arm_2025_g{g}_{n}';day=1 if n<=3 else 2;fn=f'Lucumner-Math-{g}das-HP25.pdf' if g=='7' else f'Lucumner-Math-{day}or-{g}das-HP25.pdf';src='https://olymp.am/sites/default/file/problems/'+fn;page=n if g=='7' else (n-1)%3+1
  if g=='10' and n==6:page=4
  su=src+f'#page={page}';sol='[Official worked solution (Armenian)]('+su+').';kind='source-link';notes=''
  if g=='8' and n==3:
   sol=r'Consider only the games scheduled on the final two days. They form two disjoint perfect matchings on the eight players. Their union is a collection of even cycles, with the days alternating along each cycle. Select alternate vertices of every cycle. This selects four players, no two of whom meet on either remaining day. Therefore each selected pair has already played in the first five days.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='9' and n==1:
   sol=r'The answers are $1$, the primes, and their squares. The value $1$ satisfies the condition vacuously. For composite $n$, let $p$ be its least prime factor and put $d=n/p>1$. Since $d-1\mid n-1=pd-1$, it follows that $d-1\mid p-1$, hence $d\le p$. Every prime factor of $d$ is at least $p$, so $d=p$ and $n=p^2$. Conversely a prime square has only the divisors $p,p^2$ greater than one, and $p-1\mid p^2-1$.';kind='original-exposition';notes='Includes the vacuous solution n=1, omitted in the published solution.'
  if g=='9' and n==3:
   sol=r'The maximum is $n+1$, attained by the empty set and all singletons. For each coordinate that occurs in all but at most one set, flip its membership throughout the family. This is a bijection on subsets and preserves the condition. Afterwards every element occurs in at most one member. The nonempty members are therefore disjoint, so there are at most $n$ of them, plus possibly the empty set.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='9' and n==5:
   sol=r'The maximum is **6**. At most two members can be nonpositive, since three such numbers have no positive-power sum. Among positive members, the largest has at most one partner whose sum with it is a power of two: two such sums lie strictly between the largest number and twice it, an interval containing at most one power of two. If there were five positive members, choose three not partnered with the largest. Every pair among those three would have to have a power-of-two sum, but the largest of those three cannot have two partners. Thus there are at most four positive members. The set $\{-5,-1,3,5,7,9\}$ attains six: each pair within $\{-1,3,5\}$ and within $\{-5,7,9\}$ has power-of-two sum, and any three members contain two from one group.';kind='original-exposition';notes='The upper bound explicitly includes zero among the nonpositive elements; the source discussion only mentions negative numbers.'
  if g=='10' and n==3:
   sol=r'The answer is $n\ge m$ for odd $m$, and $n\ge m-1$ for even $m$. Distinct row counts force $n\ge m-1$. If odd $m>1$ had $n=m-1$, the row counts would be $0,1,\ldots,m-1$, forcing every column count to be $m/2$, impossible. For odd $m$, construct an $m\times m$ board as follows. Make one row full; for each $i=1,\ldots,(m-1)/2$, use a pair of complementary rows containing $i$ and $m-i$ queens. Thus the row counts are $1,\ldots,m$ and every column count is $(m+1)/2$. For even $m$, use the construction for $m-1$ and add an empty row. Finally, to extend any construction with column count $k$ by one column, put queens in that new column in the $k$ rows with largest counts. Those rows all increase by one and the other rows stay unchanged, preserving distinctness.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='10' and n==6:notes='The last displayed angle in the official solution is a label typo: its two summands combine to angle XYM, as required, rather than angle XAM.'
  if g=='11-12' and n==4:
   sol=r'The only triple is $(4,4,4)$. One pair sum is even, so every factor must be a power of two. Let $2^k$ be the greatest common divisor of $a,b,c$ and divide them by it. The resulting numbers are all odd, because an odd-plus-even pair sum would be an odd power of two greater than one. Two of the three odd numbers have the same residue modulo 4; their sum is a power of two congruent to 2 modulo 4, so both equal 1. Write the third as $2^t-1$, $t\ge1$. The remaining factor is $2^k(2^t+1)+116$. For $k=0,1$, its exact power of two divisor is respectively $1,2$; for $k\ge3$ it is 4. In each case the factor is larger, impossible. Thus $k=2$, and after division by 4 the factor becomes $2^t+30$. For $t\ge2$ this is congruent to 2 modulo 4 and exceeds 2. Hence $t=1$, yielding $(4,4,4)$, which works.';kind='original-exposition';notes='Original exposition supplies the valuation step; the official text has a numerical typo in that step.'
  if g=='11-12' and n==2:notes='For n=1, the maximum is 1 and must be handled separately from the source construction; the general value is floor(n squared / 2)+1.'
